Wow.  I had never before taken this sort of approach to living forever
seriously.  Normally you assume that A) the monsters have a chance to
kill you, and therefore eventually will, and B) there is a chance of a
long period with no food generation, and therefore eventually you will
starve.  However, with guaranteed to-hit rolls and minimum damage, it
may be possible to kill enemies without any chance of their maximum
damage outpacing your minimum heal rate.  And with an icebox/ice
floor, it may be possible to accumulate corpses in such a way that the
probability of being fed forever is greater than zero.  If you can
prove both of these, then you have a way to live forever.

I see three main problems to solve.  First, it's tricky to model the
worst case for damage.  Assuming you eat rings of increase damage, I
think you can get guaranteed instant kills on fire giants.  However,
depending on the level layout, it will be possible for many giants to
approach you at once.  Mjolnir isn't guaranteed to return to you, and
has a risk of exploding your =oSD, so I think we can rule it out as a
possibility.  I think the potentially useful ranged monster attacks
have timeouts or hunger costs.  I don't think there are any other
infinitely repeatable means of killing at distance, so we're left with
melee.  You could use terrain to cut down on your exposure, but
eventually you have to go out for food, so we'd best assume you're
largely exposed to attack from multiple sides.  Elbereth might come in
handy here.  You could permanently elbereth every square you intend to
step on.  Do fire giants come with boulders?  If so, you need a
repeatable way to dispose of used boulders faster than they are
generated.

Second, you need to model the food rate.  You need to determine the
probability of getting a fire giant corpse on an accessible ice square
on any given turn.  That likely will be the same as the fire giant
generation rate long term, but you need to make sure you can always
avoid the monster generation code failing (likely due to not having a
valid place to put the monster).  You also need to make sure you can
kill giants on your ice squares and retrieve their corpses every time
without exposing yourself more than you accounted for in part 1.  From
there, it is just a calculation to determine if the odds of being fed
forever are greater than zero.  And I bet they probably are.

The third, (and I expect largest) problem is with the game code
itself.  I think there is a limit to the number of objects on a level,
and if you reach it, you won't be able to stockpile more giant
corpses.  The food calculations only work if you can stockpile an
arbitrary amount of food.  If there is a limit, no matter how high
that limit, then the probability of using up all your food at any
given time is always greater than some constant, and therefore, you
will eventually starve.  I don't think there's a solution to this
problem.

